Chapter 5B research completed without scoring or researching another chapter.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: 2022 Article IV Consultation","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-04","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2022/11/04/mexico-2022-article-iv-consultation-press-release-and-staff-report-525448","claim":"Mexico recovered more gradually than peers, while inflation accelerated and long-standing low growth and inequality constrained the economic outlook.","locator":"Summary, lines 261-264","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-001","canonical_fact_key":"imf|2022-article-iv|summary|low-growth-inflation-inequality","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.7","5B.10"],"source_type":"multilateral_economic_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"IMF country assessment published during the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly evaluates Mexico’s 2022 economic conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Provides context for national outcomes under AMLO; attribution to the president is partial because external and structural constraints were substantial.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes gradual recovery and improving labor conditions."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.7","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Staff Concluding Statement of the 2022 Article IV Mission","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-10-07","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/10/07/cs1072022-mexico-staff-concluding-statement-of-the-2022-article-iv-mission","claim":"The IMF judged the broadly neutral fiscal stance appropriate, while noting that minimum-wage increases and untargeted fiscal support helped protect incomes; it also identified weak productivity, corruption, crime, infrastructure bottlenecks, and labor/product-market rigidities as unresolved growth constraints.","locator":"Lines 259-265 and 291-293","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-002","canonical_fact_key":"imf|2022-mission|fiscal-stance-productivity-constraints","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.6","5B.7","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"],"source_type":"multilateral_policy_assessment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct IMF mission assessment with explicit policy evaluation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published during 2022 and addresses 2022 policy.","ruler_attribution":"Federal fiscal choices and wage/social-policy decisions are attributable to the administration; productivity and crime constraints are only partly attributable.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF credited recovery, remittances, and improving labor conditions alongside government measures."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.6","5B.7","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Key Policy Insights: OECD Economic Surveys: Mexico 2022","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-02-09","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_2e1de26c-en/full-report/component-4.html","claim":"Mexico’s limited tax base, weak automatic stabilizers, and absence of a federal unemployment-benefit system constrained fiscal capacity, while stronger revenue, human-capital, and infrastructure policy were recommended.","locator":"Lines 4792-4795 and 5274-5275","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-003","canonical_fact_key":"oecd|mexico-2022-survey|fiscal-capacity-human-capital","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"],"source_type":"multilateral_economic_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"OECD country survey with comparative fiscal and structural analysis.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 survey covering the target period and policy setting.","ruler_attribution":"Some constraints were inherited; the administration controlled tax, spending, human-capital, and infrastructure choices.","contrary_evidence":["The report recognized Mexico’s existing fiscal framework and debt constraints rather than attributing all weaknesses to AMLO."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: Mexico 2022","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-02-09","url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2022/02/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_a2937d37/2e1de26c-en.pdf","claim":"The OECD reported that inflation was elevated but expectations remained anchored, and emphasized preserving Banco de México’s legal autonomy and inflation-targeting framework.","locator":"PDF pp. 39-41, lines 2296-2315","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-004","canonical_fact_key":"oecd|mexico-2022-survey|banxico-autonomy-inflation-targeting","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"],"source_type":"multilateral_economic_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"OECD analysis based on Banco de México and comparative macroeconomic data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in 2022 and evaluates the active monetary-policy environment.","ruler_attribution":"The president did not directly control Banxico’s operational rate decisions; preserving institutional autonomy was an important executive constraint and policy context.","contrary_evidence":["The administration used fiscal fuel and energy measures that affected inflation dynamics."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico Inclusive and Sustainable Economic Growth Development Policy Loan","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-03-31","url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/556411654274244589/pdf/Mexico-Inclusive-and-Sustainable-Economic-Growth-Development-Policy-Loan.pdf","claim":"The World Bank projected moderate 2022 growth, constrained private investment because of regulatory uncertainty, purchasing-power losses from inflation, and possible informality effects from repeated large minimum-wage increases; it also warned that education spending cuts could undermine social progress.","locator":"PDF pp. 18-21, paragraphs 16-20, lines 682-733","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-005","canonical_fact_key":"worldbank|mexico-dpl-2022|investment-inflation-wages-education","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"],"source_type":"multilateral_development_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank country program document with macroeconomic projections and distributional analysis.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Projections and policy assessment explicitly cover 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Regulatory uncertainty, wage policy, public investment, and education allocations were substantially within federal executive influence.","contrary_evidence":["The Bank also projected declining poverty and recognized public-investment support for recovery."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Monetary Policy Statement, 15 December 2022","publisher":"Banco de México","publication_date":"2022-12-15","url":"https://www.banxico.org.mx/publications-and-press/announcements-of-monetary-policy-decisions/%7BACF4298D-BFF0-56B9-5204-535809945352%7D.pdf","claim":"Banco de México raised its overnight rate to 10.50% as headline inflation stood at 7.80% and core inflation at 8.51%, citing persistent domestic, pandemic, and geopolitical pressures.","locator":"PDF pp. 1-2, lines 7-53","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-006","canonical_fact_key":"banxico|2022-12-15|rate-10.5-inflation-7.8","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"],"source_type":"central_bank_statement","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary monetary-policy record.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"December 2022 endpoint directly reflects the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Inflation was influenced heavily by external shocks and Banxico was autonomous; executive attribution concerns fiscal and administered-price responses rather than rate setting.","contrary_evidence":["The statement identified pandemic and geopolitical factors as major inflation drivers."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pobreza laboral, cuarto trimestre de 2022","publisher":"CONEVAL","publication_date":"2023-02-20","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/Medicion/Paginas/Pobreza_laboral_4toTrim2022.aspx","claim":"Labor poverty fell from 40.3% to 38.5% year-on-year, real per-capita labor income rose 4.3%, and employment increased by 1.7 million, but labor poverty remained above the pre-pandemic level and gender and rural-urban gaps persisted.","locator":"Lines 152-164, 172-180, and 206-217","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-007","canonical_fact_key":"coneval|itlp-q4-2022|labor-poverty-income-employment","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"],"source_type":"official_social_statistics","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official CONEVAL indicator based on INEGI employment data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly reports fourth-quarter 2022 outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Outcomes reflect recovery, wage policy, transfers, remittances, and labor-market conditions; causal attribution to AMLO is mixed.","contrary_evidence":["The national rate remained above Q1 2020, with especially high poverty in Chiapas, Guerrero, and Oaxaca."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Sistema de Indicadores sobre pobreza y género, 2016-2022","publisher":"CONEVAL","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/Medicion/Paginas/Pobreza-y-genero-en-Mexico-2016-2022.aspx","claim":"In 2022 women worked fewer paid hours, performed substantially more unpaid household work, and faced greater poverty-related restrictions in labor-market participation and access to social rights.","locator":"Lines 151-193","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-008","canonical_fact_key":"coneval|poverty-gender-2016-2022|gender-work-poverty","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.8","5B.10"],"source_type":"official_social_statistics","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official CONEVAL gender-poverty indicator system.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Includes 2022 data and directly addresses distributional outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"The data show distributional conditions during AMLO’s period but do not establish that his policies caused the gender gaps.","contrary_evidence":["The source describes structural gender divisions rather than assigning them solely to the federal government."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"United States Requests Consultations Under the USMCA Over Mexico’s Energy Policies","publisher":"Office of the United States Trade Representative","publication_date":"2022-07-20","url":"https://ustr.gov/about-us/policy-offices/press-office/press-releases/2022/july/united-states-requests-consultations-under-usmca-over-mexicos-energy-policies-0","claim":"The United States challenged measures prioritizing CFE and PEMEX, delays or denials affecting private energy companies, and a June 2022 gas-network policy favoring the state firms as inconsistent with USMCA market-access, investment, and state-owned-enterprise obligations.","locator":"Paragraphs beginning “Specifically,” and lines 65-77","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-009","canonical_fact_key":"ustr|2022-usmca-consultations|mexico-energy-state-firms","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.7","5B.10"],"source_type":"foreign_government_trade_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary record of a formal USMCA consultation request, though it represents the U.S. legal position.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The challenged June and 2022 measures fall directly within the target period.","ruler_attribution":"The policies were executive-led and publicly defended by AMLO; legal responsibility remained contested.","contrary_evidence":["Mexico disputed the U.S. interpretation and invoked energy sovereignty."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.7","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico’s contribution to the OECD discussion on competitive neutrality","publisher":"COFECE/OECD","publication_date":"2021-12-09","url":"https://one.oecd.org/document/DAF/COMP/GF/WD%282021%2924/en/pdf","claim":"COFECE reported a decline in federal purchases awarded through open public tender from 65% in 2017 to 40% in 2020 and warned that procurement rules and exceptions could favor selected economic agents and weaken competitive neutrality.","locator":"PDF pp. 10-12, paragraphs 38-43, lines 455-512","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-010","canonical_fact_key":"cofece|oecd-competitive-neutrality|procurement-open-tenders","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.7"],"source_type":"competition_authority_submission","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Competition-authority submission reproduced in an OECD forum, but the main quantitative comparison predates 2022.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Provides inherited institutional context relevant to 2022, not a direct 2022 outcome.","ruler_attribution":"The trend began before 2022 but continued under AMLO’s procurement framework; direct 2022 attribution is limited.","contrary_evidence":["The source also records COFECE advocacy for stronger competitive procurement rules."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nearshoring: Optimism vs. reality of the Mexican economy","publisher":"BBVA Research","publication_date":"2022-12-20","url":"https://www.bbvaresearch.com/en/publicaciones/nearshoring-optimism-vs-reality-of-the-mexican-economy/","claim":"Mexico had strong geographic and trade advantages for nearshoring but faced major infrastructure and electricity constraints that could prevent large capital inflows from translating into sustained growth.","locator":"Summary and key points, lines 131-140","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-011","canonical_fact_key":"bbva|nearshoring-2022|electricity-infrastructure-constraint","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"],"source_type":"bank_research","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporary specialist economic analysis, though not an official evaluation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in December 2022 and addresses the year’s investment opportunity.","ruler_attribution":"Infrastructure and energy-policy choices were substantially executive-controlled, while global supply-chain relocation was external.","contrary_evidence":["Mexico’s location, USMCA access, and trade integration were significant inherited advantages."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rogelio Ramírez de la O","publisher":"Presidencia de México","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/rogelio-ramirez-de-la-o/","claim":"AMLO’s finance secretary, Rogelio Ramírez de la O, held a doctorate in economics from Cambridge, a degree in economics from UNAM, and advisory experience with Mexican and foreign-invested firms.","locator":"Biography lines 17-30","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-012","canonical_fact_key":"presidencia|ramirez-de-la-o-biography|economic-credentials","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.2"],"source_type":"official_biography","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official biography establishes credentials but naturally presents the appointee favorably.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"He served as finance secretary throughout 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The appointment was directly controlled by AMLO; the biography supports professional competence but not independent delegation or policy success.","contrary_evidence":["No source located in this wave directly demonstrates the secretary’s effective autonomy from the president."],"lenses":["5B.2"]}

Reused and verified prior evidence: 2 records, including the 2022 electricity-reform defeat and the OECD 2022 structural assessment. Newly accepted evidence: 12 units.

Documents discovered: approximately 43. Opened: 30 underlying pages, reports, PDFs, or records. Accepted: 12 new units. Rejected: approximately 8 low-authority, duplicate, snippet-only, opinion, Wikipedia, or Reddit items. Access-blocked: 6 pages, including some SHCP, ASF, Diputados, and media pages returning 403, fetch errors, or cache misses.

Distinct organizations: IMF, OECD, World Bank, Banco de México, CONEVAL, INEGI, USTR, COFECE, BBVA Research, Mexico’s Presidency, and El País. Source types included multilateral reports, official statistics, central-bank records, trade/legal records, competition-authority analysis, official biography, bank research, and journalism.

Lens coverage: all ten selected lenses have evidence. Residual weaknesses are:

- 5B.2: credentials are documented, but empowerment and independence of economic professionals remain weakly evidenced.
- 5B.5: evidence covers procurement and state-firm favoritism, but not a complete 2022 corruption record.
- 5B.6: infrastructure and productivity evidence is stronger than education and health implementation evidence.
- 5B.10: the inherited-to-left trajectory is partly documented, but a full counterfactual remains unavailable.

The cumulative manifest could not be written because filesystem operations failed with a sandbox/network-namespace error. The manifest entries above, together with the supplied prior entries AMLO22-R01, AMLO22-R02, AMLO22-R04–R09, and AMLO22-R12, form the cumulative fallback manifest for parent-led merge.

Another Chapter 5B-specific search wave would have material value mainly for 5B.2, 5B.5, and 5B.6; the present wave is otherwise reasonably saturated across macroeconomic management, distribution, investment conditions, and trajectory evidence.
